<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charlestown, Saint Kitts and Nevis</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charlestown is the capital of the island of Nevis in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-kitts">Saint Kitts</a> and Nevis, on its western coast. Founded by English settlers in the 17th century, it was a busy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> port and the centre of colonial government on the island. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>, later a founding father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, was born there in the 1750s.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlestown-saint-kitts-and-nevis.jpg" alt="Charlestown, Saint Kitts and Nevis" width="320" height="196" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aaron Vos · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nevis_Charlestown.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Charlestown was originally called Bath Bay, after Bath Stream, a stream noted for its curative waters. It became the chief town of Nevis after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a>, the island's first settlement, was inundated by a tidal wave in 1680. In the late 18th century it was both a naval base and a resort known for its mineral waters. At the northern end of the town stood the Bath Hotel and Spring House, which was a tourist hotel and spa during the 18th century, and whose main building was for a while occupied by government offices. In colonial times the town was protected by Fort Charles to the south and Fort Black Rocks to the north. Fire almost destroyed it in 1873.</p><p>Many of the oldest two-storey stone buildings were badly damaged over time by earthquakes, which tended to make the upper storey collapse into the lower one. This design flaw led to the common practice of building a wooden upper floor above a stone ground floor.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Charlestown lies on the leeward side of Nevis, near the southern end of Pinney's Beach, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-indies">West Indies</a>. It has a population of about 1,500 and is the main administrative and commercial centre of the island and its most concentrated residential area. It is also the hub of much of the island's transport, with a modern port where the ferries from St Kitts load and unload passengers, though it has no commercial airport. Its main industry has been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> milling.</p><p>Hamilton House, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-hamilton">Alexander Hamilton</a>, later a Founding Father of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, was born, stands in the town. Hamilton was born and spent his childhood there, and the restored stone building now holds the Museum of Nevis History on the ground floor and the Nevis Island Administration Assembly Room above. There are also the Nelson Museum and the Nevis Sport Museum. Nearby are the ruins of Montpelier Estate, where the naval commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> was married. Memorial Square commemorates the Nevisian soldiers who died in the two World Wars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charlestown, Nevis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charlestown</b> was a busy port, and the hot springs at the Bath Hotel drew wealthy visitors.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-nevis">Colony of Nevis</a></li></ul></section>
